## Part 2: What Each User Persona Will Demand

### The Student / Researcher
**What they care about:** Not losing research context, connecting ideas across papers/lectures, being ready for thesis writing.

**What would make them pay:**
- A **"Literature Node"** — import a research paper (PDF), and ClarityStack auto-extracts the key claims, methods, and limitations as nodes, then **links them relationally** to your existing notes.
- A **Citation Trail** — when writing a thesis, click any node and see "This idea came from Paper X, Section 3.2 + your discussion with Supervisor Y in March 2025."
- A **"Thesis Draft Mode"** — select 20 nodes and ask ClarityStack to draft a structured argument from them, with all sources pre-linked.

**Why they would NOT switch:** Because their 2-year research graph with 500+ decisions, papers, and debates is irreplaceable. The cost of rebuilding that context elsewhere is too high.

---

### The Software Engineer / Coder
**What they care about:** Architecture decisions, debugging context, not discussing the same thing twice in stand-ups.

**What would make them pay:**
- **GitHub Integration** — ClarityStack listens to PR comments and discussions; when it detects a design decision ("We chose REST over GraphQL because of our client constraints"), it automatically creates a node **linked to the specific PR.**
- **ADR Generation (Architecture Decision Records)** — Select a set of nodes and auto-generate a properly formatted ADR document (a standard engineers actually use) that can be committed directly to the repo.
- **Context Recall for Debugging** — A developer can type "Why did we switch the payment processor last month?" and ClarityStack surfaces the exact Slack thread, PR discussion, and KB node that led to that decision in under 3 seconds.

**Why they would NOT switch:** Their architecture history is encoded in the graph. Walking away from it means losing years of "why we built it this way" context.

---

### The Product Manager / Team Lead
**What they care about:** Alignment, decisions not getting lost, stakeholder visibility, accountability.

**What would make them pay:**
- **The "Contested" Dashboard** — PM's primary fear is invisible disagreements that explode into arguments 3 months later. If ClarityStack can surface conflicts *before* they blow up and require a specific human to resolve them, PMs will pay premium pricing.
- **One-Button Status Report** — Select a Project, click "Generate Weekly Update," get a pre-written summary of: new decisions made, risks opened this week, blockers resolved, and next open questions — all sourced from the actual graphs, not manual writing.
- **Decision Accountability** — Every node has an "Owner" field. Send a weekly email to owners of unresolved blockers: "You were assigned as owner of [Risk: Payment Gateway Latency], which has been open for 3 weeks. Please update its status."

**Why they would NOT switch:** Their compliance trail and team accountability structures are baked into ClarityStack. Switching means losing the audit trail and re-explaining the decision-making history to the new tool.

---

### The Enterprise Employee
**What they care about:** Security, compliance, integration with existing tools, not onboarding to yet another app.

**What would make them pay:**
- **SSO/SAML login** — mandatory. Enterprise IT will block the tool if employees are creating personal accounts with work emails.
- **The "Invisible Mode"** — a Slack bot that works silently in the background. Employees should not need to change their workflows. The knowledge graph should build itself from their existing communication channels.
- **Export for Compliance** — Audit packs that can be exported (PDF/JSON-LD) with full chain of custody for every decision. This is non-negotiable in regulated industries (finance, healthcare, legal).

**Why they would NOT switch:** The compliance audit trail literally cannot be recreated elsewhere.

