## Part 3: Features "Borrowed" from Existing Tools That ClarityStack Deserves

| Feature | Where It Exists Now | Why ClarityStack Needs It |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Bidirectional Node Links** | Obsidian, Roam Research | You already have KnowledgeEdges! But users need to be able to *manually* say "this decision SUPPORTS that one" right from the UI — not just via AI inference. |
| **Templates / Quick-Start Graphs** | Notion (page templates), Miro (board templates) | A "Product Sprint Retro" template, a "Research Project" template, an "Architecture Decision" template — pre-loaded with the right node types so users aren't starting from a blank slate. |
| **Daily Note / Log Mode** | Obsidian (Daily Notes), Notion's Journal | A simple "Today I worked on X, decided Y, and blocked by Z" input box that auto-sorts into the right graph context. Captures low-effort daily context without forcing structured input. |
| **AI-Powered "Remind Me Why..."** | Notion AI's "Ask Notion" feature | A chat window inside the Project. "Hey ClarityStack, why did we drop React Native in February?" It traverses the graph and gives a conversational answer with citations to the nodes. |
| **Kanban/Status Board View** | Linear, Jira, Notion | A view that shows KnowledgeNodes in columns: `OPEN RISKS`, `CONTESTED DECISIONS`, `RESOLVED`, `STALE`. This makes the graph actionable, not just informational. |
| **Version History / Time Travel** | Notion (Page history), Git | Show the graph as it was on a specific date. "What did we believe 3 months ago?" This is your biggest differentiator over every other tool and you already have the DB schema for it (`CardVersion`). |

